          "deepDive": "**Nにもまして** means *even more than N*. まして comes from 増す *to increase*, and the も signals that N itself is already high: 普段にもまして忙しい = *they are always busy, and now even busier*.\n\nTypical reference points are times and earlier states: **例年にもまして** (more than in an ordinary year), **普段にもまして**, **以前にもまして**, **前にもまして**, **従来にもまして**. With a demonstrative: **それにもまして** *even more than that*.\n\nWith question words (the book's ＊ note) it becomes a superlative:\n- **何にもまして** *above all, more than anything*.\n- **誰にもまして** *more than anyone*.\n- **いつにもまして** *more than ever / more than at any other time*.\n- **どこにもまして** *more than anywhere*.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜より**: a plain comparison. にもまして adds the feeling that the comparison point is already considerable, and sounds more formal and written.\n- **何よりも** ≈ 何にもまして; the latter is more literary.\n- **〜以上に**: close in meaning and slightly more neutral.\n\nPitfall: the reference point must be something already known or experienced — typically the past or the usual state. ✗将来にもまして makes no sense when describing the present; 従来にもまして重要になっている is correct.\n\nJLPT tip: 疑問詞＋にもまして is a favorite item — when a blank is followed by にもまして and the options are a question word vs. an ordinary word (今日／いつ, 何／誰), check whether the sentence means *more than ever* (いつ) or *more than anyone* (誰).",
